    "body": "`Cache.cleanup_cache` treated every directory entry as a finished cache file. A parallel `cython --cache` invocation writes `name.gz.tmp` or `name.zip.tmp` and then renames it. Cleanup could unlink that temporary file, and the writer then failed in `os.rename` with `FileNotFoundError`. Independent CLI builds, including those started by meson-python and scikit-build-core, share one cache directory.\n\nNames ending in `.tmp` are no longer part of size eviction. A temp whose mtime is at least a day old is removed on its own, so a crashed writer does not leave one forever. `stat` and `unlink` ignore `FileNotFoundError` when another process removes an entry during the scan. The order in which completed entries are evicted is unchanged. This does not change when cleanup runs.\n\nThe existing `du` check still returns before that scan when it reports the cache is under the size limit. On this machine `du` is absent, so the scan always ran.\n\nBefore the change, a fresh `module.c-fff.gz.tmp` was deleted together with the completed entries (`fresh_exists False`, directory empty). After it, that temp remains, the day-old temp is gone, and the completed entry is evicted. `tests/build/cache_cleanup.srctree` also covers a directory entry that disappears before `stat` and an `unlink` that raises `FileNotFoundError`. Those commands passed when run directly.\n\n`runtests.py` did not reach this test here: building `refnanny` failed at link with `LNK1104` on a long Windows path. Linux CI was not run here.\n\nCloses #7983.\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-10-23T12:09:04Z",
